## Terminal cancellation in the Target B boundary graph

The relative-chart approach still lacks actual loaded-boundary coverage.
The next investigation bootstrapped Target B from its current route map,
Sections 24–26, 36, 39–45, and the named phase-cut and dense-grid dependency
audits. The whole-problem prompt was read after the bootstrap. The
boundary-backtracking audit was inspected as audited-working rather than
imported as a canonical premise. The new proof uses Theorem 10.6's actual
physical train terminals and the canonical Section-39 crossing-measure,
circular-hull, and pinned-rigidity conclusions.

The new candidate pairs terminal ends in a graph whose vertices are actual
hull chains. Almost all trains outside the limiting atomic direction mass
have endpoint distance o(D). A bounded component of short such trains
cancels their terminal vectors exactly; its remaining atomic vectors force
its hull normal into a countable set. The limiting circular surface measure
is atomless, so the number of small components is o(D). The graph then gives
non-atomic train count >= q-o(D), improving s <= 1-y/2 to s <= 1-y.

Consequently the same canonical pure-disk source has
T-q >= sqrt(2*pi/sqrt(3))*D-o(D), with coefficient approximately 1.904626,
and T/D >= 3.809251, q/D >= 1.904626,
limsup q/T <= 0.622564. These are candidate necessary conditions, not a
global contradiction. The backward-flip/coforest rank and stressed equality
kernel are still unresolved. General topology and all C-II branches remain
open; no source outside the pure-disk scope is silently included.

