## Actual-loaded cap extraction from a quadratic harmonic grain

Run base 4ed65618; canonical base 023917ac, Audit.139. Chosen target:
C-II quadratic-core source geometry. Exact bridge attacked: supply
the cap/grain/port package assumed by Audits.136--137, then count
distinct return vertices outside a whole cropped region.
Canonical proof inputs: Sections 69, 73, 78 and 83, harmonic trapping,
actual loaded-hull order, and Audit.133's loaded-return theorem.

Section 78 supplies a quadratic coherent grain in one unloaded component
and its attachment cell. Among three attachments, some two are
nonconsecutive on the full loaded hull; their chord defines proper
force-hull caps. One side contains at least half the grain area.
Only O(D) rhombi meet the chord, so after omitting them, the perimeter
bound and isoperimetry give a quadratic coherent component in that cap.
The highest actual attachment has height at least grain_area/D.
This supplies a package for a cap and port selected by this construction,
not for a cap/port prescribed independently.

Trimming the cap away from its boundary and highest attachment yields
a complete deep/far grid. Canonical loaded-return paths, truncated at
their last visit to the entire cropped region, give Omega(D^2)
distinct internal source vertices outside that region. The whole-cell
variant places these vertices in a specified cell boundary strip.
No prescribed-cap hypothesis is needed for this selected packet on a
quadratic-core subsequence. Type-II near-port conclusions still use
their Type-II premise; uniform defect tails remain unproved.

Classification: proved candidate awaiting independent audit/promotion;
registry unreviewed and canonical=false. The new bridge does not pay
